(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-physiology-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Physiology Comprehensive Master Exam — Form B","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How do the lateral and medial superior olivary nuclei each determine the direction of a sound?","choices":["Lateral nuclei detect direction via differences in sound intensity between the two ears; medial nuclei detect direction via the time lag between signals reaching each ear","Both the lateral and medial nuclei rely exclusively on time-lag differences, with intensity differences playing no role at all","Both lateral and medial nuclei rely exclusively on intensity differences, with timing cues playing no role in either pathway","Neither the lateral nor the medial nucleus contributes to sound localisation, which instead depends entirely upon the inferior colliculus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The superior olivary nucleus divides into lateral nuclei (detect direction via differences in sound intensity between the two ears) and medial nuclei (detect direction via the time lag between acoustic signals arriving at each ear). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"By what point in development is the cochlea completely developed and at adult size?","choices":["Only fully developed by puberty, continuing to enlarge gradually throughout childhood","Not until several years after birth, once myelination of the auditory nerve is complete","The cochlea continues to grow slowly throughout adulthood, reaching final size around age 40","By the middle of gestation, with no further gain in size or shape expected after birth"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cochlea is completely developed by the middle of gestation and has reached adult size — no further gain in size or change in shape is expected after birth. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What are the two broad categories of hearing loss mentioned?","choices":["Only neurological hearing loss exists","Only conductive hearing loss exists","Conductive and neurological","Central and peripheral only, unrelated to the cochlea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hearing loss can be categorized as conductive (problems with sound transmission through the outer/middle ear) and/or neurological (problems with the cochlea or auditory nerve/pathway). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Approximately how long does the attenuation reflex take to activate?","choices":["Several seconds","Several minutes","40-80 msec","Instantaneously (0 msec)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The attenuation reflex takes about 40-80 msec to activate, meaning it provides some protection against sustained loud sounds but not against very sudden, brief ones. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How can humming reduce the perceived loudness of an unwanted external sound (e.g., someone else talking)?","choices":["Humming physically blocks the eustachian tube, reducing sound conduction by about 15 decibels","Humming activates only the tensor tympani muscle, producing negligible sound attenuation of about 2 decibels","Humming activates the stapedius reflex, which can reduce sound transmission to the cochlea by about 20 decibels","Humming has no effect on sound perception, since the stapedius reflex responds only to sounds above 90 decibels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Humming (producing your own low-frequency voice sound) works through the stapedius reflex, which can produce about a 20-decibel reduction in sound transmission to the cochlea, dampening the perceived loudness of other sounds like someone else's voice. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What three bones make up the ossicular chain connecting the tympanic membrane to the cochlea?","choices":["Malleus, incus, and stapes","Tectorial membrane, basilar membrane, and organ of Corti","Cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canal","Scala tympani, scala media, and scala vestibuli"],"answer":0,"exp":"The tympanic membrane is connected to the ossicles — malleus, incus, and stapes — which transmit vibrations from the eardrum to the oval window of the cochlea. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What happens to a hair cell when its stereocilia bend in one direction versus the opposite direction?","choices":["Only bending toward the tallest stereocilium produces any measurable receptor potential change","Bending in one direction causes depolarization; bending in the opposite direction causes hyperpolarization","Bending in either direction produces an identical hyperpolarization, with no depolarizing response","Bending has no electrical effect on hair cells unless it exceeds a fixed mechanical threshold angle"],"answer":1,"exp":"When stereocilia are bent in one direction, hair cells depolarize; when bent in the opposite direction, they hyperpolarize — this bidirectional sensitivity is the beginning of neural transduction of the auditory signal. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Approximately how many stiff, reed-like fibers project from the modiolus to make up the basilar membrane?","choices":["~300,000","~3,000","~30,000","~3 million"],"answer":2,"exp":"The basilar membrane contains approximately 30,000 fibers projecting from the modiolus (the bony center of the cochlea); these stiff, reed-like fibers vibrate at different frequencies depending on their position (place principle). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Why is most of what people perceive as \"taste\" actually derived from smell?","choices":["Because the tongue can only differentiate texture and five basic tastes (sweet, sour, bitter, salty, umami), so complex flavor perception mostly comes from olfaction","Because taste buds can detect thousands of distinct flavor compounds directly, without any contribution from olfactory receptors","Because smell and taste rely on entirely identical G-protein-coupled receptor mechanisms, converging upon the very same brainstem nucleus","Taste is entirely independent of smell in humans, relying solely on the five basic taste qualities detected by the tongue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Because the tongue can only indicate texture and differentiate between sweet, sour, bitter, salty, and umami, most of what is perceived as the sense of taste is actually derived from smell. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Approximately how many possible chemical receptor types are found in taste buds?","choices":["50","13","100","5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Taste buds contain up to 13 possible chemical receptor types, including two for sodium, two for potassium, chloride, adenosine, inosine, two sweet receptors, two bitter receptors, glutamate, and hydrogen ion. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What causes the perception of sour taste?","choices":["H+ ions from acids (e.g., citric acid, acetic acid)","Long-chain nitrogenous alkaloids like quinine","Glutamate and related amino acid compounds","Ionized sodium and potassium salts mainly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sour taste is caused by H+ ions from acids such as citric acid and acetic acid, with a threshold for citric acid of about 2 mM. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What chemical is primarily responsible for umami taste, and what does \"umami\" mean?","choices":["Quinine; umami means \"bitter\" in Japanese","Sucrose; umami means \"sweet\" in Japanese","Sodium chloride; umami means \"salty\" in Japanese","Glutamate; umami means \"delicious\" in Japanese"],"answer":3,"exp":"Umami taste (Japanese for \"delicious\") is primarily caused by glutamate, with a threshold for MSG (monosodium glutamate) of less than 10 mM. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is preload, and what is it proportional to?","choices":["The intrinsic contractility of the myocardium, independent of any change in ventricular volume or filling pressure","The degree of passive tension on the ventricular muscle at the end of diastole, proportional to end-diastolic volume (EDV)","The pressure the heart must pump against during systole, proportional to mean arterial pressure at the aortic valve","The heart rate at rest, proportional to background parasympathetic tone acting on the SA node"],"answer":1,"exp":"Preload of the left ventricle is the degree of passive tension on the muscle when it begins to contract at the end of diastole, proportional to end-diastolic volume (EDV) — greater preload increases actin-myosin interdigitation, producing a stronger contraction (Frank-Starling mechanism). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is afterload, and what is it proportional to?","choices":["The volume of blood remaining in the atria after ventricular contraction has finished","The heart rate alone, determined entirely by intrinsic SA node discharge frequency","The passive stretch on the atrial wall at end-diastole, proportional to atrial filling pressure","The pressure the heart must work against to eject blood during systole, proportional to mean arterial pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Afterload of the left ventricle is the pressure the heart must work against to eject blood during systole (assuming a normal aortic valve), proportional to the average (mean) arterial pressure. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What fraction of the oxygen in blood does the heart typically extract, compared to the average tissue?","choices":["The heart extracts about 20% of blood oxygen, slightly less than the average resting tissue","The heart uses 25% of blood oxygen, exactly identical to the average tissue's extraction rate","The heart uses nearly 100% of blood oxygen continuously, leaving almost none for venous return","The heart uses 70-75% of blood oxygen, compared to only about 25% for the average tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"The heart uses 70-75% of the oxygen in blood passing through it, compared to the average tissue which uses only about 25% — reflecting the heart's very high, near-maximal oxygen extraction and its heavy reliance on adequate coronary blood flow. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the primary fuel source used by the heart for energy production?","choices":["Fatty acids","Amino acids exclusively","Ketone bodies exclusively, with no other fuel used","Glucose exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Fatty acids are the primary fuel source for the heart; oxygen consumption by the heart is directly related to the amount of tension development, and is increased by increased afterload, heart size, contractility, and heart rate. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is stroke work calculated?","choices":["Stroke work = heart rate x contractility index","Stroke work = stroke volume x aortic pressure","Stroke work = end-diastolic volume x heart rate","Stroke work = ejection fraction alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Stroke work is calculated as stroke volume (SV) multiplied by aortic pressure, reflecting the mechanical work performed by the ventricle during each ejection. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is myocardial contractility, and what factors increase it (positive inotropic effect)?","choices":["A measure of heart rate alone, entirely unrelated to sarcomere length, actin-myosin overlap, or the force generated during any individual contraction","The passive stretch of the ventricle at end-diastole/actin-myosin interdigitation; increased only by preload and unaffected by any neural or hormonal factors","Contractility cannot be modified by any pharmacologic, neural, or hormonal factor whatsoever once the heart has reached full physiological maturity in adulthood","The ability to develop force at a given sarcomere length/actin-myosin interdigitation; increased by increased heart rate, sympathetic stimulation, cardiac glycosides, and increased extracellular calcium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Myocardial contractility is the ability to develop force at a given sarcomere length (a given amount of actin-myosin interdigitation); it is increased (positive inotropic effect) by increased heart rate, sympathetic stimulation, cardiac glycosides (digoxin, digitalis), and increased extracellular calcium concentration. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which of the following is used clinically as an estimate of increased contractility?","choices":["Contractility cannot be reliably estimated by any current clinical measure or imaging technique","Increased ejection fraction (SV/EDV), increased peak systolic pressure, and increased dP/dT","Decreased ejection fraction (SV/EDV) and a slower rate of pressure rise (dP/dT)","Decreased peak systolic pressure and a prolonged isovolumic contraction time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Increased contractility is estimated by increased ejection fraction (SV/EDV), increased peak systolic pressure, and increased dP/dT (or (dP/dT)/P) — the rate of pressure rise during isovolumic contraction. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the Frank-Starling mechanism (normal cardiac function curve) demonstrate, and is it dependent on innervation?","choices":["It shows that cardiac output is fixed regardless of venous return, and is entirely dependent on intact sympathetic and parasympathetic innervation","It demonstrates that contractility alone determines cardiac output, completely independent of any change in venous return or ventricular filling volume","It only applies when the heart is surgically denervated, ceasing to function properly once sympathetic and parasympathetic connections are restored","It is based on the length-tension relationship, is independent of innervation and contractility, and matches cardiac output to venous return — cardiac output can double without increasing heart rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Frank-Starling mechanism (the normal cardiac function curve) is based on the length-tension relationship, is independent of innervation and independent of contractility changes, and matches cardiac output to venous return — remarkably, cardiac output can double without any increase in heart rate, simply from increased filling (preload). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In vectorial analysis of the EKG, what does the direction of current flow in the heart represent, and what is the approximate normal mean QRS vector angle?","choices":["Current flow direction reverses randomly with each heartbeat, so no consistent mean vector can ever be defined","The mean QRS vector angle has no clinical relevance, since axis deviation never reflects underlying structural disease","Current flows from the area of depolarization to polarized areas (− to +); the normal mean QRS vector is about 59 degrees","Current flows from polarized to depolarized areas (+ to −); the normal mean QRS vector is about 0 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the heart, current flows from the area of depolarization to polarized areas (from − to +), and the resulting potential can be represented as an instantaneous mean vector; the normal mean QRS vector is approximately 59 degrees. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Why is the T wave normally positive, given that repolarization proceeds in the opposite spatial sequence from depolarization?","choices":["Because depolarization and repolarization always occur in the identical spatial sequence, with the septum both first to depolarize and first to repolarize","The T wave has no relationship to repolarization sequence, being generated instead by residual current from the preceding QRS complex","Because the last areas to depolarize (outer ventricular surface) are the first to repolarize, so repolarized areas develop a positive charge first, producing a net positive vector","T waves are always negative in a normal heart, since repolarized tissue develops a net negative charge relative to depolarized tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"The septum/endocardium are the first areas to depolarize but the last to repolarize (longer contraction duration), while the outer ventricular surface is the last to depolarize but the first to repolarize; because the first areas to repolarize develop a positive charge first, a net positive vector is generated, producing a normally positive T wave. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the functional purpose of the atrial and ventricular syncytia acting together with contraction and relaxation?","choices":["To prevent any blood flow through the heart","To keep atrial and ventricular pressures always equal","To move blood in a single direction into and out of the heart","To generate the QRS complex only, with no mechanical function"],"answer":2,"exp":"The two pumps of the heart (right, sending blood to the lungs; left, sending blood to peripheral organs) both contract and relax in a coordinated way via their syncytia to move blood through the heart in one direction. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How does the functional role of the right heart differ from the left heart?","choices":["The right heart is a volume pump; the left heart is a pressure pump","Both sides function identically as pressure pumps","Neither side generates any pressure","The right heart is a pressure pump; the left heart is a volume pump"],"answer":0,"exp":"The right heart functions primarily as a volume pump (moving the entire cardiac output to the lungs at low pressure), while the left heart functions as a pressure pump (generating the higher pressures needed for systemic circulation). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How much more distensible are veins compared to arteries?","choices":["Veins and arteries have identical distensibility","Veins are only slightly (10%) more distensible","Arteries are more distensible than veins","Veins are 8 times more distensible than arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vascular distensibility (the fractional increase in volume for each mm Hg rise in pressure) is about 8 times greater in veins than in arteries, reflecting their thinner, more compliant walls. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"How does the capacitance of veins compare to that of arteries?","choices":["Veins have about 8 times the capacitance of arteries","The capacitance of veins is about 24 times that of arteries","Arteries have roughly twice the capacitance of veins","Veins and arteries differ in capacitance by only about 10%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vascular capacitance (capacitance ≈ distensibility × volume) is about 24 times greater in veins than in arteries, allowing veins to store large quantities of blood with relatively small pressure changes and function as a reservoir. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How is pulse pressure defined and calculated?","choices":["Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic and diastolic pressure (e.g., 120 − 80 = 40 mm Hg)","Pulse pressure is the sum of systolic and diastolic pressure divided by two (e.g., 120 + 80 ÷ 2 = 100 mm Hg)","Pulse pressure is the ratio of systolic to diastolic pressure (e.g., 120 ÷ 80 = 1.5)","Pulse pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus diastolic pressure (e.g., 93 − 80 = 13 mm Hg)"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic pressure (the height of the pressure pulse, e.g., 120 mm Hg) and diastolic pressure (the lowest point, e.g., 80 mm Hg), giving a typical pulse pressure of about 40 mm Hg. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What two factors determine the degree of damping of pulse pressure as it travels into smaller peripheral arteries?","choices":["The heart rate and the total blood volume within the systemic arterial circulation","The resistance of the small vessels and arterioles, and the compliance of the larger vessels","The elasticity of the venous system and the total peripheral resistance of the capillary beds","The stroke volume ejected by the left ventricle and the resistance of the aortic valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"The degree of damping of pulse pressure in the peripheral arteries is proportional to the resistance of the small vessels/arterioles and the compliance of the larger vessels; pulsation intensity progressively decreases in smaller arteries. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What characteristic pulse pressure pattern is seen with patent ductus arteriosus?","choices":["Low systolic pressure and elevated diastolic pressure, resulting in an unusually narrow pulse pressure","Low diastolic pressure and high systolic pressure, resulting in a very high pulse pressure","Both systolic and diastolic pressures fall equally, leaving pulse pressure essentially unchanged","Systolic and diastolic pressures both rise in tandem, keeping the pulse pressure roughly normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Patent ductus arteriosus is associated with low diastolic pressure and high systolic pressure, resulting in a very high pulse pressure — reflecting the abnormal runoff of blood from the aorta into the pulmonary circulation during diastole. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What are Korotkoff sounds, and how are they used to measure systolic and diastolic pressure?","choices":["Continuous swishing sounds produced by turbulent blood flow in the brachial artery that persist at any cuff pressure above venous pressure, unrelated to systolic or diastolic values","Tapping sounds that begin when cuff pressure reaches systolic pressure, become muffled near diastolic pressure, and then disappear — the basis of the auscultatory method","Sounds that begin below diastolic pressure, grow louder as cuff pressure falls further, and disappear only once cuff pressure reaches zero","High-pitched continuous sounds heard only while the cuff is being actively inflated, disappearing immediately once cuff inflation is stopped, regardless of arterial pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Korotkoff sounds are heard via the auscultatory method: as cuff pressure falls to systolic pressure, tapping sounds begin (blood starts flowing through the partially compressed artery); as cuff pressure approaches diastolic pressure, the sounds become muffled and then disappear. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Besides the veins in general, which specific organs serve as important blood reservoirs, and which one specifically reserves red blood cells?","choices":["The lungs, kidneys, and skeletal muscle vasculature serve as reservoirs, with the kidneys specializing in platelet storage","The lungs alone serve as the body's major blood reservoir, capable of shifting large volumes into the systemic circulation","The spleen, liver, large abdominal veins, and venous plexus serve as reservoirs, with the spleen also serving as a special reservoir for red blood cells","Only the liver and kidneys serve as significant blood reservoirs, with the liver additionally storing white blood cell reserves"],"answer":2,"exp":"The spleen, liver, large abdominal veins, and the venous plexus serve as blood reservoirs, transferring blood into the arterial system as needed to maintain arterial pressure; the spleen additionally serves as a special reservoir specifically for red blood cells. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is central venous pressure, and what is its normal value?","choices":["The pressure in the left atrium, normally about 2 to 5 mm Hg at rest","The pressure in the pulmonary artery, normally about 25 mm Hg systolic","The pressure in the right atrium, normally 0 mm Hg (but can rise to 20-30 mm Hg)","The pressure in the aorta, normally about 100 mm Hg mean arterial pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Central venous pressure is the pressure in the right atrium, normally about 0 mm Hg, though it can rise as high as 20-30 mm Hg under certain conditions (e.g., heart failure, volume overload). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which of the following would tend to increase right atrial pressure (central venous pressure)?","choices":["Decreased blood volume, decreased venous tone, constriction of arterioles, or increased cardiac function","Constriction of arterioles alone, with no contribution from venous tone or blood volume","Increased blood volume, increased venous tone, dilation of arterioles, or decreased cardiac function","Decreased blood volume, dilated veins, constricted arterioles, or improved cardiac pumping ability"],"answer":2,"exp":"Right atrial pressure is regulated by the balance between the heart's ability to pump blood out and venous blood inflow; it is increased by increased blood volume, increased venous tone, dilation of arterioles (increasing venous return), or decreased cardiac function (reduced pumping ability). — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How high can venous pressure rise in the feet due to the weight of blood in a standing person, and what mechanism helps keep it lower?","choices":["Only about 25 mm Hg maximum, fully offset by baroreceptor-driven arteriolar constriction","Up to 40 mm Hg due to gravity; arterial vasoconstriction alone keeps leg venous pressure controlled","Up to 90 mm Hg due to gravity; venous valves and the \"venous pump\" help keep leg venous pressure relatively low","Up to 200 mm Hg due to gravity, with no mechanism able to lower it while standing still"],"answer":2,"exp":"The weight of blood in the vessels can cause venous pressure in the feet to rise as high as 90 mm Hg while standing; venous valves and the \"venous pump\" (from skeletal muscle contraction) help maintain relatively low venous pressure in the legs despite this gravitational effect. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What are the three ways arterial pressure (= cardiac output × total peripheral resistance) can be increased?","choices":["By dilating arterioles (decreasing TPR), dilating large veins (decreasing venous return), or directly decreasing heart rate and contractility","By constricting arterioles (increasing TPR), constricting large veins (increasing venous return/cardiac output), or directly increasing heart rate and contractility","By constricting the arterioles alone; changes in heart rate, contractility, or venous tone have no measurable effect on arterial pressure","By simultaneously dilating all arterioles and veins throughout the body, which paradoxically raises both resistance and cardiac output"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arterial pressure = cardiac output × total peripheral resistance, and can be increased by constricting arterioles (increasing TPR), constricting large veins (increasing venous return and thus cardiac output), or directly increasing cardiac output via increased heart rate and contractility. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"Where is the vasomotor center (VMC) located, and what three functional areas does it comprise?","choices":["Located bilaterally in the hypothalamus and midbrain; comprises only a vasoconstrictor and a vasodilator area","Located bilaterally in the reticular substance of the medulla and lower pons; comprises a vasoconstrictor area, vasodilator area, and sensory area","Located within the cerebellar vermis; comprises a pressor area, a depressor area, and a cardioinhibitory area","Located exclusively in the thoracolumbar spinal cord; comprises a single undifferentiated vasoconstrictor area"],"answer":1,"exp":"The vasomotor center (VMC) is located bilaterally in the reticular substance of the medulla and the lower third of the pons, and is composed of a vasoconstrictor area, a vasodilator area, and a sensory area. — 9. Circulatory Physiology I.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"Besides the Bainbridge reflex, how does direct stretch of the sinus node affect heart rate?","choices":["Stretch of the sinus node abolishes automaticity entirely, requiring backup pacemaker activity from the AV node","It has no direct effect on rhythmicity, acting only indirectly through the Bainbridge reflex pathway","Direct stretch of the sinus node can increase heart rate by as much as 10-15%, independent of the Bainbridge reflex","It only decreases heart rate, by as much as 10-15%, through direct hyperpolarization of pacemaker cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stretch of the sinus node in the wall of the right atrium has a direct effect on its own rhythmicity, increasing heart rate by as much as 10-15% — a mechanism distinct from (though complementary to) the nervous Bainbridge reflex. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"How does long-term cardiac output vary with total peripheral resistance when arterial pressure is unchanged?","choices":["Cardiac output varies inversely with TPR — increased TPR decreases cardiac output, and vice versa, when other factors are held constant","TPR has no relationship to cardiac output, since output is determined solely by heart rate and stroke volume alone","Cardiac output varies directly with TPR — increased TPR raises cardiac output proportionally when pressure is held constant","Cardiac output is completely independent of TPR, being determined instead entirely by mean systemic filling pressure alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"When arterial pressure is unchanged, long-term cardiac output varies inversely with total peripheral resistance — any change in the long-term TPR level (with no other change in circulatory function) produces a cardiac output change in exactly the opposite direction. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What two mechanisms make sympathetic stimulation cause a \"hypereffective\" heart?","choices":["It decreases heart rate to as low as 40 beats per minute while simultaneously reducing contractile strength by about half","It has no effect on cardiac contractility at all, only slowing heart rate down to about 50 to 60 beats per minute at rest","It only decreases the heart rate significantly, from 72 down to as low as 20 to 40 beats per minute, leaving contractility entirely unaffected","It greatly increases heart rate (e.g., from 72 up to 180-200 beats/min) and increases the strength of contraction (contractility) to as much as twice normal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation (with parasympathetic inhibition) creates a hypereffective heart by greatly increasing heart rate (potentially from 72 up to 180-200 beats/min in young people) and increasing contractility to as much as twice its normal strength. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What happens to cardiac output and arterial pressure during peripheral vasodilation if the sympathetic nervous system is not functioning?","choices":["Without the sympathetic nervous system, the heart cannot increase cardiac output to offset the large decrease in total peripheral resistance, so arterial pressure drops","Peripheral dilation has no effect on arterial pressure as long as renal sodium retention increases proportionally within seconds","The parasympathetic system alone fully compensates for the drop by increasing heart rate through vagal withdrawal at the sinus node","Cardiac output increases automatically through the Frank-Starling mechanism alone, and the arterial pressure returns fully to baseline within seconds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Without an intact sympathetic nervous system, the heart cannot increase cardiac output enough to offset a large decrease in total peripheral resistance from peripheral vasodilation, so arterial pressure drops significantly. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What are the three principal factors that affect venous return to the heart?","choices":["Right atrial pressure, the degree of filling of the systemic circulation (mean systemic filling pressure), and resistance to blood flow between peripheral vessels and the right atrium","Only heart rate and stroke volume, since venous return is entirely and exclusively determined by the heart's own pumping activity alone","Only the right atrial pressure, since mean systemic filling pressure and venous resistance both remain essentially constant under all physiological conditions","Only the resistance of the pulmonary circulation, since systemic venous resistance has essentially no bearing on flow into the right atrium at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"The three principal factors affecting venous return are: right atrial pressure (a backward force impeding flow into the atrium), the degree of filling of the systemic circulation (mean systemic filling pressure, forcing blood toward the heart), and resistance to blood flow between the peripheral vessels and right atrium. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the mean systemic filling pressure, and what is its typical value?","choices":["The equilibrium pressure throughout the systemic circulation when all blood flow ceases, about 7 mm Hg","The peak systolic pressure recorded within the right ventricle, normally about 25 mm Hg","A theoretical pressure that only applies to the pulmonary circulation, never the systemic circulation","The pressure within the coronary sinus during diastole, normally about 5 mm Hg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Mean systemic filling pressure is the equilibrium pressure that arterial and venous pressures reach throughout the systemic circulation when all blood flow ceases, normally about 7 mm Hg; it increases as blood volume increases and as sympathetic stimulation constricts the vasculature (decreasing its capacity). — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What proportion of the total resistance to venous return is attributable to venous resistance versus arteriolar/small artery resistance?","choices":["Venous resistance contributes essentially none, with nearly all resistance from small arteries","About two-thirds is venous resistance, about one-third is arteriolar/small artery resistance","About one-third is venous resistance, about two-thirds is capillary resistance","Both contribute equally, with each accounting for roughly half of total resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"About two-thirds of the resistance to venous return is determined by venous resistance, and about one-third is determined by arteriolar and small artery resistance. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In the graphical analysis of cardiac output during heavy exercise, how does the equilibrium point shift?","choices":["Right atrial pressure falls dramatically to negative values as venous return increases during exercise","Cardiac output increases substantially (e.g., to about 20 L/min) as exercise increases both the cardiac function curve and venous return curve","Exercise has no effect on the venous return curve, only shifting the cardiac function curve upward alone","Cardiac output decreases from about 5 L/min down to roughly 2 L/min as the vessels constrict throughout exercising muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"During heavy exercise, both the cardiac function curve and the venous return curve shift, moving the equilibrium point (where venous return equals cardiac output) from a normal resting value of about 5 L/min to as high as about 20 L/min. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Using the Fick principle, if oxygen absorption by the lungs is 200 mL/min and the arteriovenous oxygen difference is 40 mL/L, what is the calculated cardiac output?","choices":["2 L/min","40 L/min","8000 L/min","5 L/min"],"answer":3,"exp":"By the Fick principle, cardiac output = oxygen absorbed by the lungs / arteriovenous oxygen difference = 200 mL/min ÷ 40 mL/L = 5 L/min, matching the normal resting cardiac output. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"By approximately how many times can skeletal muscle blood flow increase from rest to extreme exercise?","choices":["2-3x","100x","12-25x","No increase occurs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Skeletal muscle blood flow can increase from about 3-4 mL/min/100g at rest to 50-80 mL/min/100g during extreme exercise — a 12-25 fold increase, driven by local metabolite-triggered vasodilation and opening of previously dormant capillaries. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is functional hyperemia?","choices":["A decrease in blood flow triggered by sympathetic vasoconstriction during rest","An increase in blood flow seen only within resting cardiac muscle tissue","A pathological increase in blood flow occurring only during sleep","The increase in blood flow that occurs when a muscle becomes active"],"answer":3,"exp":"Functional hyperemia is the increase in blood flow that occurs when a muscle becomes metabolically active, driven by local metabolites (lactic acid, ATP, K+, CO2) triggering vasodilation in response to reduced local oxygen availability. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the role of sympathetic vasoconstrictor nerve activation during exercise, given that it can attenuate local vasodilation?","choices":["It has no functional role during exercise, since sympathetic outflow to skeletal muscle vasculature is completely suppressed once exercise starts","It acts as a \"brake\" preventing excess dilation in exercising muscle (triggered by metabolic factors), while also constricting other vascular beds and non-exercising muscle to redistribute blood flow","It only affects the coronary and cerebral vasculature, having essentially no effect whatsoever upon skeletal muscle or the splanchnic vessels during exertion","It completely overrides all local metabolic vasodilation, fully shutting down blood flow to exercising muscle no matter how high oxygen demand rises"],"answer":1,"exp":"Sympathetic vasoconstrictor nerve activation can attenuate (but not eliminate) the local metabolic vasodilation in exercising muscle, acting as a \"brake\" against excess dilation, while simultaneously constricting other vascular beds and non-exercising muscle to redistribute blood flow to where it's needed most. — Circulatory Physiology II.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Which photoreceptor type provides color vision, and which provides higher visual acuity?","choices":["Cones are trichromatic (three pigment types) and provide color vision with high acuity due to less convergent retinal pathways; rods are achromatic (one pigment, rhodopsin) with lower acuity due to highly convergent pathways","Rods are trichromatic (three pigment types) and provide color vision with high acuity due to less convergent retinal pathways concentrated in the fovea; cones are achromatic (a single pigment) with lower acuity due to highly convergent pathways","Rods provide higher acuity than cones due to less convergent retinal pathways concentrated centrally in the fovea; cones are achromatic, using a single pigment type distributed diffusely throughout the peripheral retina","Both rods and cones are trichromatic, each containing three distinct photopsin pigments sensitive to different wavelengths; acuity differs only because cones cluster densely within the fovea while rods dominate the periphery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cones are trichromatic (three types, each with a different photopsin pigment) and provide color vision along with high acuity due to less convergent retinal pathways (concentrated in the fovea); rods are achromatic (a single pigment, rhodopsin) with lower acuity due to highly convergent retinal pathways, and are more common in the periphery. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the function of the melanin-containing pigment layer of the retina, and what happens in albinism?","choices":["It produces rhodopsin directly for the photoreceptors and has no role at all in preventing light scatter; albinos therefore show markedly reduced rhodopsin synthesis","It has a purely nutritive role supplying glucose to photoreceptors; albinism has no visual consequences since this layer is unaffected","It prevents light reflection/scattering within the eye, maintaining contrast; albinos lack this pigment layer and have poor visual acuity due to light scattering, rarely correctable better than 20/200","It only matters for color perception, not acuity; albinos retain normal visual acuity but lose red-green wavelength discrimination"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pigment layer of the retina contains melanin, which prevents light reflection/scattering within the globe of the eye and maintains contrast; albinos lack this pigment layer, causing diffuse light scattering and poor visual acuity, rarely correctable to better than 20/200 even with lenses. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What happens when a photon of light is absorbed by the retinal molecule within rhodopsin?","choices":["Retinal changes configuration from 11-cis to all-trans, breaks away from rhodopsin (activating it), which then triggers a second-messenger cascade closing Na+/Ca2+ channels and causing hyperpolarization","The photon directly opens Na+ channels without any second messenger, producing an immediate depolarization lasting milliseconds","Absorption of light causes depolarization rather than hyperpolarization, as retinal shifts from the all-trans form back to 11-cis and opens cation channels in the membrane","Retinal remains unchanged, and rhodopsin is not activated; instead the photon energy is absorbed directly by the opsin backbone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Absorption of a photon by the 11-cis retinal within rhodopsin causes it to change configuration to all-trans retinal, which breaks away from and activates rhodopsin (forming Metarhodopsin II); this triggers a second-messenger cascade leading to closure of Na+/Ca2+ channels and hyperpolarization of the photoreceptor. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the pigment protein found in cones called, and how many types exist?","choices":["Iodopsin; only two types exist in cones, sensitive to blue and yellow wavelengths","Scotopsin; two types exist, sensitive to red and green wavelengths","Melanopsin; four types exist, each tuned to a different visible wavelength","Photopsin; three types exist (sensitive to red, green, and blue wavelengths)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The pigment protein in cones is called photopsin, a close analog of rhodopsin; there are three types of photopsin (types I, II, and III), each sensitive to a different part of the visible spectrum (red, green, and blue respectively), giving trichromatic color vision. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What two components make up rhodopsin, and what configuration of retinal is required to bind scotopsin?","choices":["Only opsin protein, with retinal bound irreversibly regardless of its configuration","Melanopsin and iodopsin, requiring the trans configuration of retinal to bind","Scotopsin (a protein) and retinal (a pigment); only the cis configuration of retinal can bind scotopsin to form rhodopsin","Photopsin together with melanin, requiring either the cis or the trans configuration of retinal in order to bind"],"answer":2,"exp":"Rhodopsin (visual purple) is a combination of scotopsin (a protein) and retinal (a pigment); only the cis configuration of retinal can bind with scotopsin to form rhodopsin — light exposure converts it to the trans configuration, which cannot bind scotopsin, causing the retinal to split away. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why does vitamin A deficiency cause night blindness (nyctalopia)?","choices":["Vitamin A deficiency causes excess rhodopsin production within the rod outer segments, which paradoxically desensitizes photoreceptors and disrupts the normal cycle of dark adaptation over several weeks","Vitamin A1 (all-trans retinal) is required to regenerate 11-cis retinal for rhodopsin production; deficiency decreases retinal availability, decreasing rhodopsin production and reducing retinal light sensitivity","Vitamin A deficiency only affects cone function, not rods, reducing daytime color discrimination while leaving nighttime scotopic sensitivity essentially unaffected by the underlying deficiency","Vitamin A has no direct relationship to retinal photopigments; night blindness instead results from reduced blood flow to the optic nerve head during prolonged dim-light exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vitamin A1 is converted to 11-cis retinal within the retinal pigment epithelium, needed to regenerate rhodopsin; a deficiency (e.g., from GI absorption problems like celiac disease or cholestasis, since vitamin A is fat-soluble) decreases retinal availability, decreasing rhodopsin production and reducing retinal sensitivity to light — causing night blindness. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the global significance of vitamin A deficiency in children?","choices":["It only causes minor, easily reversible vision changes, resolving within days once dietary vitamin A intake is restored","It is the leading cause of preventable childhood blindness worldwide in developing countries, causing an estimated 250,000-500,000 children to become blind each year","It exclusively affects adults, not children, since children's higher metabolic vitamin A turnover protects against deficiency","It has no significant public health impact at all, since fortified foods across the developing world have largely eliminated deficiency blindness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Vitamin A deficiency is the leading cause of preventable childhood blindness worldwide in developing countries, with an estimated 250,000-500,000 children becoming blind each year, with highest prevalence in Southeast Asia and Africa; prolonged severe deficiency can cause total, irreversible blindness. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Why does an object come into better focus as it moves closer to the eye in a person with myopia (nearsightedness)?","choices":["Myopia has no relationship to focal length, since the elongated eyeball only affects peripheral, not central, image formation","Myopia affects only colour perception and not focus distance, because the elongated eyeball alters wavelength refraction rather than the focal point itself","Distant objects always focus better in myopic eyes than near objects, since a longer eyeball shortens the needed focal length","A point source of light has a longer focal length than light from a distant source, which is why closer objects (acting more like point sources) come into focus more easily in myopic (elongated) eyes"],"answer":3,"exp":"A point source of light has a longer focal length compared to light from a distant source; this is why an object comes into focus as it moves closer to the eye in a person with myopia (nearsightedness, associated with an elongated eyeball). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What are the two dietary forms of vitamin A, and what is the most common plant-based (pro-vitamin A) source?","choices":["Vitamin A cannot be obtained from any dietary source and must instead be synthesized endogenously by the liver from cholesterol","Only pro-vitamin A exists, found exclusively in dairy products such as milk and cheese, with no preformed vitamin A in the diet","Only preformed vitamin A exists, found exclusively in animal products, since plants cannot produce carotenoid precursors","Preformed vitamin A (from animal products like meat, fish, dairy) and pro-vitamin A (from plant foods, most commonly beta-carotene)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Vitamin A comes in two dietary forms: preformed vitamin A (from animal products such as meat, fish, poultry, and dairy) and pro-vitamin A (from plant-based foods such as fruits and vegetables), with beta-carotene being the most common pro-vitamin A source. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What type of lens is generally used to correct myopia, based on the eye's need to diverge incoming light?","choices":["A concave lens, to diverge light rays before they reach the eye's own overly strong focusing power","Only bifocal lenses can correct myopia, since single-vision lenses cannot diverge light enough for an elongated eyeball","A convex lens, to further focus light and move the focal point even farther in front of the retina than it already sits","No lens correction is possible for myopia; only surgical shortening of the eyeball's axial length restores normal focus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Since myopia results from the eye's optical system focusing light in front of the retina (excessive focusing power for its axial length), a concave (diverging) lens is used to correct it by diverging light rays before they enter the eye, moving the focal point back onto the retina. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How does the adjustability of the lens compare to that of the cornea in terms of refractive power?","choices":["The lens has less overall refractive power than the cornea, but its power is adjustable (via accommodation), while the cornea's refractive power is fixed","Neither structure's refractive power is adjustable; total power instead varies only with pupil diameter and ambient light","The cornea's power is adjustable via accommodation, ranging from 20 to 34 diopters, while the lens's power remains essentially fixed","The lens contributes more total refractive power than the cornea, accounting for roughly two-thirds of the eye's focusing ability"],"answer":0,"exp":"The lens has less refractive power than the cornea overall, but its power is adjustable via accommodation (ranging from about 20 to 34 diopters), while the cornea's refractive power (about two-thirds of the eye's total) is essentially fixed. — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the two fluid-filled chambers of the eye, and how do they differ in flow characteristics?","choices":["Vitreous humor is in front of the lens and freely flowing; aqueous humor is behind it, forming a gelatinous, slow-flowing mass","Only one fluid chamber exists in the eye, since the anterior and posterior segments share a single continuous fluid space","Aqueous humor (in front of the lens, freely flowing) and vitreous humor (behind the lens, a gelatinous mass with little fluid flow)","Both chambers contain identical freely flowing fluid that circulates continuously between the anterior and posterior segments"],"answer":2,"exp":"The eye has two fluid chambers: aqueous humor in front of the lens (a freely flowing fluid) and vitreous humor behind the lens (a gelatinous mass with little fluid flow). — Physiology of Vision.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
